        "result": "Three EXACT, verified facts give the CRT/halving lens real teeth on the single-deletion core layer [1,13]\\{e} (target 1/14). (1) RIGID ODD BASE: for every even e, the odd speeds of [1,13]\\{e} are the SAME set {1,3,5,7,9,11,13}, with CONSTANT lonely measure L_odd=75454/315315=0.23929721. The odd speeds carry ZERO discrimination between cores; all variation in L lives in the even/doubling content. (2) DYADIC HALVING (the literal \"2\" in 14=2·7): adding the even speeds to the odd base by 2-adic level halves the measure — odd base 0.23930 → +level1{2,6,10}=0.11729 (ratio 0.490≈1/2) → +level2{4,12}=0.04519 → +level3{8}=0 (= consec, L=0). The doubling map z↦2z that halves band widths is the same one that (order 3 mod 7) splits sectors into QR/NQR. (3) CLEAN CRT PRODUCT for the minimizer: with the cell split τ=a/7+ξ/7, L(drop-6)=(#surviving cells/7)·R=(2/7)·(49/1716)=7/858 — a 7-adic factor (pure residue-mod-7: only mirror cells a=1,6 survive) times an archimedean within-cell density R=49/1716 (equal on both by τ↔1−τ). Drop-6 is the UNIQUE core minimizing BOTH CRT factors at once. Mechanism for why drop-6 wins: its 4 critical intervals are bounded by {11,12,13} with perfectly balanced parity (4 even boundaries ALL owned by 12=2·6, 4 odd owned by 11,13); deleting 6=2·3 is cheapest because its dyadic-tower parent 12=2²·3 survives to re-cover 6's bands. ALL 5 even deletions beat ALL 7 odd deletions — the odd base is the rigid skeleton, evens are refinements. CRT clean-product is SPECIAL (holds only for low-surviving-cell cores drops 1,2,3,4,6; fails for high-L cores).",
